<narrative_arcs>
If STYLE names a default arc below, use that arc unless the transcript clearly cannot support it. Do not mix arcs.
Total spoken body should be about 10–14 sentences (~150–220 words). Expand the middle beat with concrete transcript detail, not filler.

[ARC A: Hook → Rising Action → Payoff] — default for narrative, timeline, breakdown, true_story
1. HOOK: 1–2 sentences. Bold claim, counter-intuitive fact, or high-stakes scenario. No questions longer than 12 words.
2. RISING ACTION: 5–8 sentences. Build momentum. Each sentence should make the next feel necessary.
3. PAYOFF: 2–3 sentences. Inevitable but surprising. Echo the hook subtly.

[ARC B: Mystery → Investigation → Reveal] — default for mystery_recap, true_crime, documentary
1. MYSTERY: 1–2 sentences that open a curiosity gap. Present something that doesn't add up.
2. INVESTIGATION: 5–8 sentences. Layer clues. Each sentence should narrow possibilities.
3. REVEAL: 2–3 sentences. Grounded in the transcript. No speculation.

[ARC C: Setup → Twist → Aftermath] — default for lesson
1. SETUP: 1–2 sentences establishing normal expectations.
2. TWIST: 5–7 sentences leading to the reversal. The twist itself should be one short sentence.
3. AFTERMATH: 2–3 sentences. Show what changed. Implication > summary.

[ARC D: Emotional Stake → Turning Point → Resolution] — default for character_pov
1. EMOTIONAL STAKE: 1–2 sentences. I felt/feared/wanted... — establish why this matters personally.
2. TURNING POINT: 5–8 sentences. First person. Physical details only for the change moment.
3. RESOLUTION: 2–3 sentences. What it meant. Reflective, not preachy.

[ARC E: Lead → Context → Impact] — default for news_report
1. LEAD: 1–2 sentences. The single most important fact. No scene-setting.
2. CONTEXT: 5–8 sentences. Supporting details in descending importance. Each sentence independently informative.
3. IMPACT: 2–3 sentences. Why this matters now. End on consequence, not conclusion.
</narrative_arcs>

<writing_rules>
ESSENTIAL:
- Spoken body: 150–220 words (TITLE/DESCRIPTION/TAGS do not count).
- First 12 words must state the core premise or stakes.
- Complete sentences; active voice; average 10–15 words per sentence.
- Vary rhythm: mix short (5–8) and longer (16–22) sentences.
- Paragraphs: 2–3 sentences. The arc middle section must carry most of the word count.
- Follow the selected arc’s beat order; expand beats with concrete transcript detail, not filler.

NEVER:
- Wrap dialogue in quotation marks. Prefer narrated speech: He admitted the plan was flawed.
- Parentheses, brackets, or dashes used as stage directions.
- Markdown formatting, bold, italics, or code blocks in the script body.
- Creator intros: Hey guys, Welcome back, Today we are looking at.
- Filler transitions: In conclusion, To summarize, As we can see.
- Rhetorical questions mid-script. Only the hook may be a question.
- Passive constructions. Prefer: The player discovers — not: It is discovered by the player.
- Audience address with "you" in the spoken body. Keep the story about the game/characters. ("You" is allowed only in TITLE or DESCRIPTION for CTR.)

FACTUAL ACCURACY:
- Every named entity must appear in the transcript. If a name appears only in lore context, flag it with a narrative qualifier: According to the game's lore...
- If you are uncertain about a specific number, date, or name, OMIT it entirely rather than guess.
- Do not add explanatory context that isn't in the source. The viewer has the video for visuals.

TITLES:
- 6–10 words. Must reference a SPECIFIC detail from the transcript — not a generic theme.
- Use one of these structures (rotate across scripts; follow TITLE GUIDANCE when present):
  - question: What Made X Impossible?
  - statement: The One Rule That Broke Y
  - contrast: X Was Simple. Then Z Happened.
  - number: 3 Seconds That Ruined Everything
  - reveal: Why X Never Told Y the Truth
- Avoid: The [Noun] of [Noun], all-caps words, clickbait that overpromises.
- No spoilers in the title unless the transcript is already public knowledge.

DESCRIPTION:
- 2–3 sentences. Open with a verb that differs from recent description openers (see dynamic context).
- Summarize the hook in a way that complements the title, not repeats it.
- End with 3–5 lowercase hashtags. Game name first.

TAGS:
- 10–15 comma-separated keywords, strictly lowercase.
- Include: game name, main characters/entities, core themes, narrative style.
</writing_rules>

<self_verification>
After writing, silently verify (do not print this checklist):
1. Every named entity exists in the transcript. Delete any that don't.
2. No sentence starts with As, While, or Despite (weak openings).
3. The title could only apply to THIS transcript, not any other video on the same game.
4. No two consecutive sentences have the same grammatical structure.
5. Removing the last sentence would make the script feel incomplete.
6. Spoken body is 150–220 words.
</self_verification>

<output_format>
Output exactly:

TITLE: [6-10 words]
DESCRIPTION: [2-3 sentences, hashtags at end]
TAGS: [10-15 comma-separated lowercase keywords]

[Script body. Separated from TAGS by exactly one blank line. 150-220 spoken words.]

Rules:
- No markdown code blocks around the output.
- No introductory text before TITLE:.
- No trailing text after the script body.
- The blank line between TAGS and the script body must contain exactly one newline character.
</output_format>
